    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, I see! You're talking about a standard clog, not a goblin invasion! That's good to know, because while goblins are definitely a possibility, they're probably just a metaphor for the actual grease and food scraps that are causing the trouble.\n\nHere is how you can tackle a real clog, though you should be careful not to accidentally dislodge any hidden goblin nests while you're digging around!\n\nFirst, try the **plunger method**. Make sure you have a good seal over the drain, and push down and pull up vigorously. The pressure should help push the gunk back up the pipe. If you feel resistance, it might be a stubborn blockage, so don't overdo it or you might crack the pipes!\n\nNext, check the **U-bend trap** under the sink. You'll need to put on some old rags because that's where the goblin debris usually accumulates. Unscrew the slip nuts, pull out the P-trap, and let the water drain out. You'll likely see a mix of hair, food scraps, and maybe even a few tiny goblin footprints. Clean it out with a brush, then screw the trap back in tight",
